            "scheme": "file"
          }
        },
        {
          "value": " files, you can write a script to merge them programmatically. Here's an example in PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\n# Define paths to the launch.json files\n$files = @(\n    \"path\\to\\launch1.json\",\n    \"path\\to\\launch2.json\"\n)\n\n# Initialize an empty array for configurations\n$configurations = @()\n\n# Loop through each file and extract configurations\nforeach ($file in $files) {\n    $json = Get-Content $file | ConvertFrom-Json\n    $configurations += $json.configurations\n}\n\n# Create the merged launch.json\n$mergedLaunch = @{\n    version = \"0.2.0\"\n    configurations = $configurations\n}\n\n# Save the merged launch.json\n$mergedLaunch | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10 | Set-Content \"path\\to\\merged\\launch.json\"\n```\n\n### 5. **Validate the Merged File**\nAfter merging, ensure the resulting ",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
