            {
              "code": "{\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    // Configurations from File 1\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch App 1\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/app1.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    },\n    // Configurations from File 2\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch App 2\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/app2.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n",
              "language": "jsonc",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "To combine multiple  files into a single list of launch configurations, you can follow these steps:\n\n### 1. **Understand the Structure**\nEach  file contains a `configurations` array that holds individual launch configurations. To merge multiple files, you need to combine all the `configurations` arrays into one.\n\n### 2. **Manually Merge Configurations**\nCopy the `configurations` array from each  file and paste them into a single `configurations` array in the target  file. Ensure there are no duplicate entries or conflicting names.\n\nFor example:\n\n"
            },
            {
              "code": "{\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    // Configurations for Project A\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch Project A\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/projectA/index.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    },\n    // Configurations for Project B\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch Project B\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/projectB/index.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n",
              "language": "jsonc",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "\n### 3. **Use Comments for Organization**\nIf you are merging configurations from different projects or purposes, use comments to separate and document them.\n\n"
            },
            {
              "code": "# Define paths to the launch.json files\n$files = @(\n    \"path\\to\\launch1.json\",\n    \"path\\to\\launch2.json\"\n)\n\n# Initialize an empty array for configurations\n$configurations = @()\n\n# Loop through each file and extract configurations\nforeach ($file in $files) {\n    $json = Get-Content $file | ConvertFrom-Json\n    $configurations += $json.configurations\n}\n\n# Create the merged launch.json\n$mergedLaunch = @{\n    version = \"0.2.0\"\n    configurations = $configurations\n}\n\n# Save the merged launch.json\n$mergedLaunch | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10 | Set-Content \"path\\to\\merged\\launch.json\"\n",
              "language": "powershell",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "\n### 4. **Automate with a Script (Optional)**\nIf you have many  files, you can write a script to merge them programmatically. Here's an example in PowerShell:\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [
            {
              "role": "user",
              "content": "how are multiple launch.json files into a single list of launch configurations?"
            },
            {
              "role": "assistant",
              "content": "To combine multiple `launch.json` files into a single list of launch configurations, you can follow these steps:\n\n### 1. **Understand the Structure**\nEach `launch.json` file contains a `configurations` array that holds individual launch configurations. To merge multiple files, you need to combine all the `configurations` arrays into one.\n\n### 2. **Manually Merge Configurations**\nCopy the `configurations` array from each `launch.json` file and paste them into a single `configurations` array in the target `launch.json` file. Ensure there are no duplicate entries or conflicting names.\n\nFor example:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    // Configurations from File 1\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch App 1\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/app1.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    },\n    // Configurations from File 2\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch App 2\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/app2.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### 3. **Use Comments for Organization**\nIf you are merging configurations from different projects or purposes, use comments to separate and document them.\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    // Configurations for Project A\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch Project A\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/projectA/index.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    },\n    // Configurations for Project B\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Launch Project B\",\n      \"type\": \"node\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${workspaceFolder}/projectB/index.js\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### 4. **Automate with a Script (Optional)**\nIf you have many `launch.json` files, you can write a script to merge them programmatically. Here's an example in PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\n# Define paths to the launch.json files\n$files = @(\n    \"path\\to\\launch1.json\",\n    \"path\\to\\launch2.json\"\n)\n\n# Initialize an empty array for configurations\n$configurations = @()\n\n# Loop through each file and extract configurations\nforeach ($file in $files) {\n    $json = Get-Content $file | ConvertFrom-Json\n    $configurations += $json.configurations\n}\n\n# Create the merged launch.json\n$mergedLaunch = @{\n    version = \"0.2.0\"\n    configurations = $configurations\n}\n\n# Save the merged launch.json\n$mergedLaunch | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 10 | Set-Content \"path\\to\\merged\\launch.json\"\n```\n\n### 5. **Validate the Merged File**\nAfter merging, ensure the resulting `launch.json` file is valid JSON and works as expected in Visual Studio Code. You can use the built-in JSON validation in VS Code or an online JSON validator.\n\n### 6. **Use Workspace-Specific Launch Configurations**\nIf you want to keep configurations separate but accessible, you can use [multi-root workspaces](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/multi-root-workspaces) and define separate `launch.json` files for each folder."
            }
          ],
