        "metadata": {
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "code": "{\n  \"folders\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ATAP.Utilities\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n",
              "language": "json",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "Visual Studio Code's **\"Run and Debug\"** dropdown shows configurations from multiple  files because it aggregates all  files present in the  directories of the workspace folders. This behavior is expected when using a multi-root workspace or when multiple  directories exist in the project structure.\n\n### How to Resolve or Manage This Behavior:\n\n#### 1. **Verify Workspace Settings**\nCheck if your workspace is a **multi-root workspace**. If so, VS Code will include configurations from all  files in the workspace folders.\n\n- Open the workspace settings file (`.code-workspace`) if it exists.\n- Ensure only the required folders are included in the `folders` array.\n\nExample `.code-workspace` file:\n"
            },
            {
              "code": "\"compounds\": [\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Main Utilities Debug\",\n    \"configurations\": [\"Run Extension (Development)\", \"PowerShell Launch Current File\"]\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"BuildTooling Debug\",\n    \"configurations\": [\"PowerShell Launch Current File with -WhatIf\"]\n  }\n]\n",
              "language": "json",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "\nIf you don't want configurations from , remove its entry from the `folders` array.\n\n#### 2. **Use `compounds` to Group Configurations**\nIf you want to manage configurations from multiple  files, you can use the `compounds` property in the main  file to group and organize them.\n\nExample:\n"
            },
            {
              "code": "{\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    // Configurations from ATAP.Utilities\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Run Extension (Development)\",\n      \"type\": \"extensionHost\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"args\": [\"--extensionDevelopmentPath=${env:VSCExtensionProjectAbsolutePath}\"],\n      \"outFiles\": [\"${env:VSCExtensionProjectAbsolutePath}/_generated/development/**/*.js\"]\n    },\n    // Configurations from ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\n    {\n      \"name\": \"PowerShell Launch Current File\",\n      \"type\": \"PowerShell\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"script\": \"${file}\",\n      \"args\": [\"-verbose\"],\n      \"cwd\": \"${file}\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n",
              "language": "json",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "\nThis allows you to select grouped configurations in the dropdown.\n\n#### 3. **Disable Unwanted Configurations**\nIf you don't want configurations from a specific  file to appear, you can:\n- Remove or rename the  file in the unwanted  directory.\n- Alternatively, move the configurations to a single  file in the root  directory.\n\n#### 4. **Manually Merge Configurations**\nIf you want all configurations in one place, merge the configurations from both  files into a single file in the root  directory.\n\nExample:\n"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [
            {
              "role": "user",
              "content": "Visual studio code 'Run and debug' drop-down is shoing configurtions from two different launch.json files 'ATAP.Utilities\\.vscode\\launch.json',  'ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildToolinmg.Powershell\\.vscode\\launch.json'"
            },
            {
              "role": "assistant",
              "content": "Visual Studio Code's **\"Run and Debug\"** dropdown shows configurations from multiple `launch.json` files because it aggregates all `launch.json` files present in the `.vscode` directories of the workspace folders. This behavior is expected when using a multi-root workspace or when multiple `.vscode` directories exist in the project structure.\n\n### How to Resolve or Manage This Behavior:\n\n#### 1. **Verify Workspace Settings**\nCheck if your workspace is a **multi-root workspace**. If so, VS Code will include configurations from all `.vscode/launch.json` files in the workspace folders.\n\n- Open the workspace settings file (`.code-workspace`) if it exists.\n- Ensure only the required folders are included in the `folders` array.\n\nExample `.code-workspace` file:\n```json\n{\n  \"folders\": [\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ATAP.Utilities\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"path\": \"ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nIf you don't want configurations from `ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell/.vscode/launch.json`, remove its entry from the `folders` array.\n\n#### 2. **Use `compounds` to Group Configurations**\nIf you want to manage configurations from multiple `launch.json` files, you can use the `compounds` property in the main `launch.json` file to group and organize them.\n\nExample:\n```json\n\"compounds\": [\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Main Utilities Debug\",\n    \"configurations\": [\"Run Extension (Development)\", \"PowerShell Launch Current File\"]\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"BuildTooling Debug\",\n    \"configurations\": [\"PowerShell Launch Current File with -WhatIf\"]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nThis allows you to select grouped configurations in the dropdown.\n\n#### 3. **Disable Unwanted Configurations**\nIf you don't want configurations from a specific `launch.json` file to appear, you can:\n- Remove or rename the `launch.json` file in the unwanted `.vscode` directory.\n- Alternatively, move the configurations to a single `launch.json` file in the root `.vscode` directory.\n\n#### 4. **Manually Merge Configurations**\nIf you want all configurations in one place, merge the configurations from both `launch.json` files into a single file in the root `.vscode` directory.\n\nExample:\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    // Configurations from ATAP.Utilities\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Run Extension (Development)\",\n      \"type\": \"extensionHost\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"args\": [\"--extensionDevelopmentPath=${env:VSCExtensionProjectAbsolutePath}\"],\n      \"outFiles\": [\"${env:VSCExtensionProjectAbsolutePath}/_generated/development/**/*.js\"]\n    },\n    // Configurations from ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\n    {\n      \"name\": \"PowerShell Launch Current File\",\n      \"type\": \"PowerShell\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"script\": \"${file}\",\n      \"args\": [\"-verbose\"],\n      \"cwd\": \"${file}\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n#### 5. **Use Workspace-Specific Debugging**\nIf you want to keep configurations separate but accessible, you can create **workspace-specific debugging** by defining a `.code-workspace` file and switching between workspaces as needed.\n\n#### 6. **Restart VS Code**\nAfter making changes to the `launch.json` files or workspace settings, restart Visual Studio Code to ensure the changes are applied.\n\nBy following these steps, you can control which configurations appear in the **\"Run and Debug\"** dropdown and manage multiple `launch.json` files effectively."
            }
          ],
