      "response": [
        {
          "value": "The `Get-Module -ListAvailable` cmdlet searches for modules in the directories specified in the `$env:PSModulePath` environment variable. If your local source directory is not included in `$env:PSModulePath`, `Get-Module` will not find your module.\n\n### Steps to Fix the Issue:\n\n#### 1. **Check `$env:PSModulePath`**\nRun the following command to see the directories currently included in `$env:PSModulePath`:\n\n```powershell\n$env:PSModulePath -split ';'\n```\n\nThis will list all the directories where PowerShell looks for modules.\n\n#### 2. **Add Your Local Source to `$env:PSModulePath`**\nIf your local source directory is not listed, you need to add it. For example, if your module is located in ",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
            "$mid": 1,
            "path": "/c:/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/",
            "scheme": "file"
          }
        },
        {
          "kind": "inlineReference",
          "inlineReference": {
            "$mid": 1,
            "fsPath": "c:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\\",
            "_sep": 1,
            "external": "file:///c%3A/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell/",
            "path": "/C:/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/src/ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell/",
            "scheme": "file"
          }
        },
        {
          "value": ", you can add it like this:\n\n```powershell\n$env:PSModulePath += \";C:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\"\n```\n\n#### 3. **Verify the Module is Found**\nAfter adding the directory, run the following command to verify that the module is now found:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Module -ListAvailable -Name 'ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.Powershell'\n```\n\n#### 4. **Make the Change Permanent**\nThe above change is temporary and will only last for the current PowerShell session. To make it permanent, you can add the directory to `$env:PSModulePath` in your PowerShell profile script.\n\n- Open your PowerShell profile script:\n  ```powershell\n  notepad $PROFILE\n  ```\n\n- Add the following line to the script:\n  ```powershell\n  $env:PSModulePath += \";C:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\"\n  ```\n\n- Save and close the file. The change will take effect in all future PowerShell sessions.\n\n#### 5. **Alternative: Import the Module Directly**\nIf you don't want to modify `$env:PSModulePath`, you can import the module directly using its path:\n\n```powershell\nImport-Module \"C:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.Powershell.psd1\"\n```\n\nThis will load the module for the current session without modifying `$env:PSModulePath`.\n\nBy following these steps, `Get-Module -ListAvailable` should be able to find your local module.",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
            "$mid": 1,
            "path": "/c:/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/",
            "scheme": "file"
          }
        }
      ],
      "responseId": "response_2303c034-f577-4826-bb64-568bba8a2fde",
      "result": {
        "timings": {
          "firstProgress": 2784,
          "totalElapsed": 12433
        },
        "metadata": {
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "code": "$env:PSModulePath -split ';'\n",
              "language": "powershell",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "The `Get-Module -ListAvailable` cmdlet searches for modules in the directories specified in the `$env:PSModulePath` environment variable. If your local source directory is not included in `$env:PSModulePath`, `Get-Module` will not find your module.\n\n### Steps to Fix the Issue:\n\n#### 1. **Check `$env:PSModulePath`**\nRun the following command to see the directories currently included in `$env:PSModulePath`:\n\n"
            },
            {
              "code": "$env:PSModulePath += \";C:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\"\n",
              "language": "powershell",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "\nThis will list all the directories where PowerShell looks for modules.\n\n#### 2. **Add Your Local Source to `$env:PSModulePath`**\nIf your local source directory is not listed, you need to add it. For example, if your module is located in , you can add it like this:\n\n"
            },
            {
              "code": "Get-Module -ListAvailable -Name 'ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.Powershell'\n",
              "language": "powershell",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "\n#### 3. **Verify the Module is Found**\nAfter adding the directory, run the following command to verify that the module is now found:\n\n"
            },
            {
              "code": "Import-Module \"C:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.Powershell.psd1\"\n",
              "language": "powershell",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "\n#### 4. **Make the Change Permanent**\nThe above change is temporary and will only last for the current PowerShell session. To make it permanent, you can add the directory to `$env:PSModulePath` in your PowerShell profile script.\n\n- Open your PowerShell profile script:\n  ```powershell\n  notepad $PROFILE\n  ```\n\n- Add the following line to the script:\n  ```powershell\n  $env:PSModulePath += \";C:\\Dropbox\\whertzing\\GitHub\\ATAP.Utilities\\src\\ATAP.Utilities.BuildTooling.PowerShell\"\n  ```\n\n- Save and close the file. The change will take effect in all future PowerShell sessions.\n\n#### 5. **Alternative: Import the Module Directly**\nIf you don't want to modify `$env:PSModulePath`, you can import the module directly using its path:\n\n"
            }
          ],
