      "response": [
        {
          "value": "To get the number of objects in an object of type `[System.Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentBag[string]]`, you can use the `.Count` property. Here's an example:\n\n```powershell\n$filesToBeProcessed = [System.Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentBag[string]]::new()\n$filesToBeProcessed.Add(\"File1.txt\")\n$filesToBeProcessed.Add(\"File2.txt\")\n\n# Get the count of objects in the ConcurrentBag\n$filesToBeProcessed.Count\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- `[System.Collections.Concurrent.ConcurrentBag[string]]` is a thread-safe collection.\n- The `.Count` property provides the number of items in the collection.\n\nIn your script, you can use:\n\n```powershell\nWrite-Host \"Number of files to be processed: $($filesToBeProcessed.Count)\"\n```",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
