      "response": [
        {
          "value": "The output of `Get-PSFLoggingProvider -Name gelf | Format-List` indicates that the GELF logging provider is **enabled** but **not initialized** (`Initialized: False`). This means that while the provider is configured and enabled, it has not yet been fully set up to start logging.\n\n### **Steps to Resolve the Issue**\n\n1. **Explicitly Initialize the Logging Provider**\n   Use the `Wait-PSFMessage` cmdlet to initialize the logging provider. This ensures that the provider is ready to handle log messages.\n\n   ```powershell\n   $null = Wait-PSFMessage -Timeout 1\n   ```\n\n   After running this, check the status again using:\n\n   ```powershell\n   Get-PSFLoggingProvider -Name gelf | Format-List\n   ```\n\n   The `Initialized` property should now be `True`.\n\n---\n\n2. **Verify the Configuration**\n   Ensure that all required configuration settings for the GELF provider are correctly set. For example:\n\n   ```powershell\n   Set-PSFConfig 'PSFramework.Logging.gelf.GelfServer' '127.0.0.1'\n   Set-PSFConfig 'PSFramework.Logging.gelf.Port' 12201\n   Set-PSFConfig 'PSFramework.Logging.gelf.Protocol' 'tcp'\n   Set-PSFConfig 'PSFramework.Logging.gelf.Encrypt' $false\n   Set-PSFConfig 'PSFramework.Logging.gelf.Enabled' $true\n   ```\n\n   After setting these, verify the configuration:\n\n   ```powershell\n   Get-PSFConfig -FullName 'PSFramework.Logging.gelf.*' | Format-List\n   ```\n\n---\n\n3. **Check for Dependencies**\n   Ensure that the target GELF server (e.g., Graylog or SEQ) is running and accessible at the specified `GelfServer` and `Port`. If the server is not reachable, the provider may fail to initialize.\n\n   - Test connectivity to the server:\n     ```powershell\n     Test-NetConnection -ComputerName 127.0.0.1 -Port 12201\n     ```\n\n---\n\n4. **Enable Verbose Logging for Debugging**\n   Enable verbose logging to get more details about the initialization process:\n\n   ```powershell\n   Set-PSFConfig 'PSFramework.Logging.gelf.Verbose' $true\n   ```\n\n   Then, monitor the output for any errors or warnings during initialization.\n\n---\n\n5. **Restart the Logging Provider**\n   If the provider still does not initialize, try restarting it:\n\n   ```powershell\n   Set-PSFLoggingProvider -Name gelf -Enabled $false\n   Set-PSFLoggingProvider -Name gelf -Enabled $true\n   ```\n\n   Then, reinitialize it:\n\n   ```powershell\n   $null = Wait-PSFMessage -Timeout 1\n   ```\n\n---\n\n6. **Check for Errors**\n   If the provider still fails to initialize, check for any errors in the PowerShell session or logs. Use the following command to capture any error messages:\n\n   ```powershell\n   Get-PSFMessage -Level Error\n   ```\n\n---\n\n### **Expected Outcome**\nAfter following these steps, the `Get-PSFLoggingProvider -Name gelf | Format-List` output should show:\n\n```plaintext\nInitialized                : True\n```\n\nThis indicates that the GELF logging provider is fully initialized and ready to log messages.",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
