        "metadata": {
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "code": "# 1. Check out your bug branch\ngit checkout bug-branch\n\n# 2. Push it to origin and set the remote tracking branch\ngit push --set-upstream origin bug-branch\n",
              "language": "bash",
              "markdownBeforeBlock": "To get your local bug-fix branch up into origin so you can merge it, push it and set its upstream first:\n\n"
            }
          ],
          "toolCallRounds": [
            {
              "response": "To get your local bug-fix branch up into origin so you can merge it, push it and set its upstream first:\n\n````bash\n# 1. Check out your bug branch\ngit checkout bug-branch\n\n# 2. Push it to origin and set the remote tracking branch\ngit push --set-upstream origin bug-branch\n````\n\nOnce it’s on origin you have two main options:\n\n1. **Merge locally**  \n   ```bash\n   git checkout main\n   git pull origin main\n   git merge bug-branch\n   git push origin main\n   ```\n\n2. **Open a Pull Request on GitHub**  \n   - In VS Code’s Source Control panel click **Publish Branch** (or use `git push` above).  \n   - Go to your repo on GitHub and click **Compare & pull request**.  \n   - Review, merge, then pull `main` back down:  \n     ```bash\n     git checkout main\n     git pull origin main\n     ```\n\nEither approach will bring your fully-committed, local bug-fix branch back into `main`.",
