            "kind": "promptFile",
            "modelDescription": "Prompt instructions file",
            "isRoot": true,
            "originLabel": "Automatically attached as setting github.copilot.chat.codeGeneration.useInstructionFiles is enabled",
            "automaticallyAdded": true
          }
        ]
      },
      "response": [
        {
          "value": "Here’s how to squash-and-merge your GitHub pull request entirely from within VS Code’s Source Control / GitHub PR UI:\n\n1. Install & Sign In  \n   - Make sure you have the **GitHub Pull Requests and Issues** extension installed.  \n   - Click the 🔑 in the status bar to sign into GitHub.\n\n2. Open the GitHub PR View  \n   - In the Activity Bar, click the GitHub icon (Pull Requests).  \n   - Under **PULL REQUESTS**, find your bug-fix branch’s PR.\n\n3. Review & Complete  \n   - Click your PR to open its details.  \n   - At the top of the PR pane, click **Complete** (▼) and select **Squash merge**.\n\n4. Edit the Commit Message  \n   - In the “Squash and merge” dialog that appears, enter a single descriptive commit message (e.g.  \n     `Fixes #123: Correct GUID formatting`).  \n   - Click **Squash and merge** to finish.\n\n5. Clean Up  \n   - After merge completes, click **Delete branch** in the confirmation banner (if you no longer need it).\n\nThat’s it—no CLI required. Your PR will be squashed into one commit on `main`, the GitHub issue will auto-close, and you’ll have cleaned up the branch.",
          "supportThemeIcons": false,
          "supportHtml": false,
          "baseUri": {
            "$mid": 1,
            "path": "/c:/Dropbox/whertzing/GitHub/ATAP.Utilities/",
            "scheme": "file"
          }
        }
      ],
      "responseId": "response_6486e5c5-ac5d-4a80-939e-d069851d9d69",
